From: Fexiven <48439988+Fexiven@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 11:58:11 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 21/23] rocket: rk3576: CONCATENATION at any channel offset

The zero-copy concatenation needed every input but the last to have a
multiple of 16 channels, so YOLOX's three detection-head concatenations
(4 + 1 + 80 channels) stayed on the CPU.

Every input now starts at a 16-channel group of the concatenation's
buffer. When the inputs are not all multiples of 16 (except the last),
that buffer is an internal tensor of ALIGN(c_0, 16) + ALIGN(c_1, 16) +
... channels, the producers still write their views of it, and a 1x1
convolution with 0/1 weights packs the channels into the real output.
The vendor compiler does the same for such offsets (DPU copies into a
padded buffer and a convolution "restride"); the inputs and the output
share their quantization (TFLite's int8 kernel requires it), so the
packing convolution's requantization is an exact 1.0. It is a normal
convolution for the two-core planner and the job batching. Only int8
tensors take this path, as only they were tested.

An input of <= 4 channels is claimed only when a claimed operation of
the partition writes it: a subgraph input of that size would be uploaded
in the tight first-convolution layout.

count_tensors() now counts every concatenation input and accepts PAD,
which reached its UNREACHABLE default.

YOLOX: 13 -> 16 concatenations on the NPU, 16.4 -> 16.0 ms, outputs
byte-identical; test models with 4 + 1 + 80, 24 + 40 + 8, 3 + 5 + 20 and
1 + 17 + 2 + 30 channels, as graph outputs and feeding a 3x3
convolution, are byte-identical to the CPU concatenation in one- and
two-core mode.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01C6ragSxZN7WKWgGsfgrMX1
